{
  "requirement": "Before presenting, the `habeas-requirements` skill MUST direct agents to apply the revised text the reviews return. Where reviews conflict, for example two reviews that split the same requirement differently, it MUST direct them to reconcile the conflict, and to present as a design question any conflict whose resolution changes what is promised.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The requirement is about the content of the `habeas-requirements` skill. It has three obligations: (1) direct agents to apply the revised text the reviews return before presenting, (2) direct them to reconcile conflicting reviews, and (3) direct them to present as a design question any conflict whose resolution changes what is promised.\n\nThe test is `tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[the_requirements_skill_applies_and_reconciles_reviews_before_presenting]`. It covers each obligation:\n- **Apply the revised text.** `step_of(SKILL, \"Apply the revised text the reviews return\")` panics unless some workflow step contains that phrase.\n- **Before presenting.** `assert!(apply < step_of(SKILL, \"Present each group to the user\"))` checks that the apply step is numbered before the step that presents.\n- **Reconcile clashes.** `assert_text` on `step(SKILL, apply)` requires the phrase \"reconcile reviews that clash, such as two that split the same requirement differently\". This covers the named example of a split requirement.\n- **Design question.** The same `assert_text` call requires \"a clash whose resolution changes what is promised becomes a design question\".\n\nThe skill text in `assets/skills/habeas-requirements/SKILL.md`, in the Workflow section's review step, contains all of these phrases. The helpers `step_of`, `step` and `assert_text` do real assertions: they panic or assert when a phrase is missing. A textual check suits a requirement about a document's content. Every clause is asserted in the right step, and nothing is delegated to another requirement. No acknowledgments apply, and I found no gap.",
